-- Glasshouse v0: the needle. One trade, one official curve, one MTM
-- that is only admissible against an official curve, one correction
-- that supersedes. This file is the rule model the commit layer runs;
-- everything else in Glasshouse (API, projections, imports, screens)
-- is machinery around what these rules admit.
--
-- Three Glasshouse laws are structural here, not conventions:
--
--   * The organisation is the tenancy boundary. Every predicate leads
--     with its org; isolation is in the shape of the facts, not in a
--     WHERE clause someone remembers to add.
--   * Authority is governed capability claims. Who may capture, who
--     may publish curves, who may admit valuations - each is a claim
--     in the ledger, granted by a transition, auditable and as-of
--     queryable like everything else. The app maps identity to an
--     actor string and nothing more.
--   * Supersede, never overwrite. A curve correction registers a new
--     version alongside the old, links the lineage, and moves the
--     official pointer. The old figure, and any valuation that relied
--     on it, stays on the record. This is machine-enforced, not
--     conventional: the record predicates are `append only` (a
--     retract is a static authoring error), and the official pointer
--     is a declared `current pointer` with its supersession lineage.
--
-- Claim-shape law (identity uniqueness, the singleton pointer, no-fork
-- lineage, effective dating, append-only records) is declared on the
-- predicates as disciplines; morpholog generates and enforces the
-- invariants (and, for effective dating, the in-force selector) and
-- `inspect guarantees` traces each back to its clause. Only
-- referential and value law remains authored by hand below.
--
-- Bulk curve content never enters the ledger: a curve version carries
-- the hash of its price array, and the array itself lives in the app
-- schema keyed by the registered identity (the read-side law: every
-- app table is a projection or a hash-anchored payload).
--
-- Delivery periods are UTC instants (Timestamp), never local civil
-- time: DST days fall out of timezone arithmetic in the compute zone
-- and are never special-cased here.
--
-- Patterns that read only some of a wide claim's fields name them
-- (`TradeTerms(quantity: qty, ..)`) rather than counting underscores to
-- the right position. This is parse-time sugar - a named pattern and
-- its positional twin share a canonical hash, so the rules identity
-- pinned as MODEL_HASH is unaffected - and it buys the thing counting
-- cannot: reordering a predicate's fields can no longer silently bind
-- the wrong one. Where a hole is the point rather than an omission, it
-- is named too, so the reader sees the coordinate deliberately left
-- free instead of inferring it from a gap.

program glasshouse

-- The vocabulary. `TradeCaptured` is the trade's immutable identity
-- (direction and counterparty included: those are what the trade IS);
-- its economic terms live in `TradeTerms`, versioned the way the curve
-- is versioned rather than ever edited. Each version carries the
-- business date it takes effect from, and `effective by (trade) on
-- (effective_from)` makes morpholog write the selector every temporal
-- programme used to hand-roll: `trade_terms_in_force_on(trade, as_of,
-- ..)` names the version whose date is the latest not after `as_of`.
-- An amendment is a new version alongside the old with its lineage
-- linked in `TradeTermsSupersedes`; the old terms, and any valuation
-- computed under them, stay on the record. `CurveRegistered` is a
-- curve version's identity including the hash anchoring its payload;
-- `OfficialCurve` is the current pointer naming the version in force
-- for a market and as-of date, superseded along the `CurveSupersedes`
-- lineage. `TradeValued` records one mark-to-market result, pinned to
-- the exact curve version AND the exact terms version it was computed
-- against, so neither a curve correction nor an amendment ever
-- silently re-writes a number anyone saw.
--
-- The capability predicates carry no discipline on purpose: they are
-- only ever admitted in v0, and revocation, when it arrives, is a
-- governed retraction that `append only` would forbid statically.
--
-- Units are contractual labels on exact decimals: a quantity is
-- `Decimal[MW]`, an MTM is `Decimal[EUR]`, and mixing them is
-- structurally uncommittable. The price is deliberately a bare
-- Decimal: EUR/MWh is a ratio - a field name and a formula in the
-- compute zone, never a unit (compound symbols are not units here).
predicate MayCaptureTrade(actor: Subject, org: Subject, book: Subject)
predicate MayRegisterCurve(actor: Subject, org: Subject, market: Subject)
predicate MayValueTrade(actor: Subject, org: Subject, book: Subject)
predicate TradeCaptured(org: Subject, book: Subject, trade: Subject, counterparty: Subject, market: Subject, direction: Subject)
    unique by (trade)
    append only
predicate TradeTerms(org: Subject, trade: Subject, version: Subject, quantity: Decimal[MW], price: Decimal, delivery_start: Timestamp, delivery_end: Timestamp, effective_from: Date)
    unique by (version)
    effective by (trade) on (effective_from) partial
    append only
predicate TradeTermsSupersedes(new_version: Subject, prior_version: Subject)
    unique by (prior_version)
    append only
predicate CurveRegistered(org: Subject, market: Subject, as_of: Date, version: Subject, payload_hash: Subject)
    unique by (version)
    append only
predicate OfficialCurve(org: Subject, market: Subject, as_of: Date, version: Subject)
    current pointer by (org, market, as_of)
    superseded via CurveSupersedes
predicate CurveSupersedes(new_version: Subject, prior_version: Subject)
    append only
predicate TradeValued(org: Subject, book: Subject, trade: Subject, curve_version: Subject, terms_version: Subject, mtm: Decimal[EUR])
    unique by (trade, curve_version, terms_version)
    append only

-- The referential and value rules that must always hold, by any path.
-- (Uniqueness, the official-pointer singleton and no-fork lineage are
-- generated from the discipline clauses above.)

-- Terms belong to a captured trade, in the same organisation.
invariant terms_belong_to_captured_trade:
    TradeTerms(org: org, trade: trade, ..) implies TradeCaptured(org: org, trade: trade, ..)

-- A trade for nothing, or for negative power, is not a trade.
invariant quantity_is_positive:
    TradeTerms(quantity: qty, ..) implies qty > 0 MW

-- A delivery period is a real interval on the timeline.
invariant delivery_period_is_ordered:
    TradeTerms(delivery_start: delivery_start, delivery_end: delivery_end, ..)
    implies delivery_start strictly_before delivery_end

-- The official pointer can never name a version that was not
-- registered for exactly that org, market and as-of date.
invariant official_curve_is_registered:
    OfficialCurve(org, market, as_of, version)
    implies CurveRegistered(org: org, market: market, as_of: as_of, version: version, ..)

-- A valuation belongs to a captured trade, in the same org and book.
invariant valued_trade_was_captured:
    TradeValued(org: org, book: book, trade: trade, ..)
    implies TradeCaptured(org: org, book: book, trade: trade, ..)

-- A valuation's curve version exists in the same organisation. (That
-- it was *official at admission time* is the gate's job, checked when
-- the valuation is admitted and not retroactively disturbed by later
-- corrections - a valuation is a record of what was computed, not a
-- promise the curve stays current.)
invariant valuation_curve_is_registered:
    TradeValued(org: org, curve_version: version, ..)
    implies CurveRegistered(org: org, version: version, ..)

-- A valuation's terms version is a registered version of that trade,
-- in the same organisation.
invariant valuation_terms_version_is_registered:
    TradeValued(org: org, trade: trade, terms_version: version, ..)
    implies TradeTerms(org: org, trade: trade, version: version, ..)

-- Lineage never crosses trades: a version amends a version of the
-- same trade.
invariant terms_lineage_stays_within_the_trade:
    TradeTermsSupersedes(new_version, prior_version)
    and TradeTerms(org: org, trade: trade, version: new_version, ..)
    implies TradeTerms(org: org, trade: trade, version: prior_version, ..)

-- That a mark's terms version was *in force on the curve's date at
-- admission time* is the gate's job, exactly as officialness is for
-- the curve: a valuation is a record of what was computed, and an
-- amendment effective on a date already marked supersedes the mark
-- by re-marking, never by making the old record unlawful. (A record
-- invariant saying "every mark names the terms in force on its
-- curve's date" was tried and refused every intra-day amendment of a
-- marked trade; it is the analogue of "every mark names the official
-- curve", which this model deliberately does not have. The
-- effective-dated predicate is therefore declared `partial`: where no
-- version is in force the selector matches nothing, and the gate is
-- what refuses a mark there.)

-- The only ways state may change.

-- Authority grants are ungated in v0: bootstrap seeds capabilities at
-- organisation setup. Maker-checker on grants is a v1 two-step
-- transformation pattern, deliberately deferred - the grant is already
-- a governed, attributable, as-of-queryable transition.
transformation grant_capture_authority(principal, org, book):
    admit MayCaptureTrade(principal, org, book)

transformation grant_curve_authority(principal, org, market):
    admit MayRegisterCurve(principal, org, market)

transformation grant_valuation_authority(principal, org, book):
    admit MayValueTrade(principal, org, book)

-- Capture a trade: identity and the first version of its terms in one
-- transition, effective from the trade date. The gate ties the actor's
-- capability to this org and this book; the trade id is fixed once
-- captured (a change of terms is an amendment, never an overwrite).
-- The version id is the caller's, like a curve version's.
transformation capture_trade(org, book, trade, counterparty, market, direction, version, quantity, price, delivery_start, delivery_end, trade_date):
    require MayCaptureTrade(actor, org, book)
    require not TradeCaptured(trade: trade, ..)
    require not TradeTerms(version: version, ..)
    admit TradeCaptured(org, book, trade, counterparty, market, direction)
    admit TradeTerms(org, trade, version, quantity, price, delivery_start, delivery_end, trade_date)

-- Amend the economic terms the honest way: a new version alongside the
-- old, lineage linked, effective strictly after the version it amends,
-- so the chain, the timeline and the log agree on which terms are
-- current. Direction and counterparty are identity and stay on
-- `TradeCaptured`. The capture capability for the trade's book is the
-- amendment capability: whoever may book a trade may re-book its terms.
transformation amend_trade(org, trade, prior_version, new_version, quantity, price, delivery_start, delivery_end, effective_from):
    require TradeCaptured(org: org, trade: trade, book: book, ..) and MayCaptureTrade(actor, org, book)
    require TradeTerms(org: org, trade: trade, version: prior_version, effective_from: prior_from, ..)
    and effective_from after prior_from
    require not TradeTermsSupersedes(prior_version: prior_version, ..)
    require not TradeTerms(version: new_version, ..)
    admit TradeTerms(org, trade, new_version, quantity, price, delivery_start, delivery_end, effective_from)
    admit TradeTermsSupersedes(new_version, prior_version)

-- Register a curve version and make it official in one move. This is
-- the first-registration path: if a curve is already official for
-- this org, market and as-of date, the honest move is a correction,
-- which keeps lineage - so this gate refuses rather than quietly
-- replacing.
transformation register_curve(org, market, as_of, version, payload_hash):
    require MayRegisterCurve(actor, org, market)
    require not CurveRegistered(version: version, ..)
    require not OfficialCurve(org: org, market: market, as_of: as_of, version: _)
    admit CurveRegistered(org, market, as_of, version, payload_hash)
    admit OfficialCurve(org, market, as_of, version)

-- Correct an official curve the honest way: the prior version stays
-- on the record, the corrected version is registered alongside it,
-- lineage is linked, and the official pointer moves. Valuations
-- admitted under the prior version are untouched; the correction
-- governs what may be valued next.
transformation correct_curve(org, market, as_of, prior_version, new_version, payload_hash):
    require MayRegisterCurve(actor, org, market)
    require OfficialCurve(org, market, as_of, prior_version)
    require not CurveSupersedes(prior_version: prior_version, ..)
    require not CurveRegistered(version: new_version, ..)
    admit CurveRegistered(org, market, as_of, new_version, payload_hash)
    admit CurveSupersedes(new_version, prior_version)
    retract OfficialCurve(org, market, as_of, prior_version)
    admit OfficialCurve(org, market, as_of, new_version)

-- Admit a mark-to-market result. The number is computed outside (the
-- compute zone values the trade against the curve payload); the
-- ledger governs that the result is *admissible*: the actor holds the
-- valuation capability for this book, the trade exists here, and -
-- the rule this product is built around - the curve version used is
-- the one officially in force for the trade's market, at admission
-- time, and the terms version used is the one in force on that
-- curve's business date. An MTM against a stale or unofficial curve,
-- or against terms that were not in force, is structurally
-- uncommittable, not merely discouraged. The three claim patterns
-- share one `require` because bindings flow only within a
-- conjunction: the curve's `as_of` binds in the pointer and feeds the
-- selector, whose payload holes are wildcarded positionally
-- (definitions take parameters, not fields, so they cannot be named).
transformation admit_valuation(org, book, trade, curve_version, terms_version, mtm):
    require MayValueTrade(actor, org, book)
    require TradeCaptured(org: org, book: book, trade: trade, market: market, ..)
    and OfficialCurve(org: org, market: market, as_of: as_of, version: curve_version)
    and trade_terms_in_force_on(trade, as_of, org, terms_version, _, _, _, _)
    require not TradeValued(trade: trade, curve_version: curve_version, terms_version: terms_version, ..)
    admit TradeValued(org, book, trade, curve_version, terms_version, mtm)
